'''Bidau Creole Portuguese''', also known as '''Timor Pidgin'''<ref name="ISO639">{{cite web|url=http://www.lexvo.org/page/iso639-3/tvy|title=iso639-3/tvy|access-date=2024-11-19}}</ref> was a [[Portuguese Creole|Portuguese-based creole language]] that was spoken in {{ill|Bidau|tet|Bidau Lecidere}}, [[Nain Feto]], an eastern suburb of [[Dili]], [[East Timor]].

Bidau Creole Portuguese is believed to have grown out of the Portuguese spoken by settlers and ''[[Mestizo|mestiços]]'' from [[Flores|Flores Island]] (especially the [[Sikka people|Sikka]]), influenced by languages introduced to the area by [[military]] men from [[Lifau]]. It shares a number of features with nearby creoles such as [[Macanese language|Macanese]].

